.TH CHMAP_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_declare_scoped \- declare a hash map handle that is destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_declare_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro, and attaches the cleanup attribute, which destroys the map at
every exit from the enclosing scope. The macro creates nothing: the
handle holds NULL until one of the init macros runs.

It differs from chmap_declare(3) in exactly three ways: it sets the
handle to NULL instead of leaving it uninitialized, it sets both
companion type variables to NULL as well, and it gives the handle a
cleanup attribute, so an exit from the scope destroys the map that the
handle names at that moment. Because the handle starts as NULL and the
library ignores a NULL handle, a return before an init macro runs is
safe, and nothing in the block reads an indeterminate object.

Use this macro with chmap_init(3) or one of its variants when the code
decides the configuration of the map inside the scope;
chmap_construct_scoped(3) is the one-step form for a map whose
configuration is known at the declaration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key and, with
         val_t, which implementation strategy the init macro selects
val_t    Value type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
An explicit chmap_destroy(3) before the end of the scope stays correct:
it sets the handle to NULL, and the cleanup at scope exit then does
nothing
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return the handle, and do
not store it anywhere that outlives the scope: either one hands out a
pointer that is freed at the exit from the scope
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a
function that receives the handle needs chmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_declare_scoped(scores, char *, int);
if (!load_config()) {
    return -1;             /* nothing was created; cleanup is a no-op */
}
chmap_init(scores);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
return 0;                  /* scores is destroyed here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_construct_scoped (3),
.BR chmap_init (3),
.BR chmap_init_full (3),
.BR chmap_redeclare (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
